    <fr:tree show-metadata="true" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year></fr:date><fr:uri>https://mkiyoaki.github.io/ai-reasoning-verification/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>ai-reasoning-verification</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/ai-reasoning-verification/</fr:route><fr:title text="AI Reasoning Verification: Stepwise Verification via NLI">AI Reasoning Verification: Stepwise Verification via NLI</fr:title><fr:taxon>Thesis</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="institution"><fr:link href="https://www.cam.ac.uk/" type="external">University of Cambridge</fr:link></fr:meta><fr:meta name="position">Supervisor: Prof. <fr:link href="/andreasvlachos/" title="Andreas Vlachos" uri="https://mkiyoaki.github.io/andreasvlachos/" display-uri="andreasvlachos" type="local">Andreas Vlachos</fr:link></fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><html:ul><html:li>Reframed stepwise reasoning verification as local Natural Language Inference (NLI), employing fair comparison of language models with different architectures and training paradigms.</html:li>  
  <html:li>Results showed that discriminative supervision consistently outperforms generative verdict training across both encoder and decoder LMs. (Supported by the Alan Turing Institute).</html:li></html:ul></fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
  

  
    
    
    <fr:tree show-metadata="true" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year></fr:date><fr:uri>https://mkiyoaki.github.io/l101-sft-rl-mechanisms/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>l101-sft-rl-mechanisms</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/l101-sft-rl-mechanisms/</fr:route><fr:title text="Representation vs. Execution: SFT and RL under Distribution Shift">Representation vs. Execution: SFT and RL under Distribution Shift</fr:title><fr:taxon>Mini Project</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="institution"><fr:link href="https://www.cam.ac.uk/" type="external">University of Cambridge</fr:link> (L101)</fr:meta><fr:meta name="position">Supervisor: Prof. <fr:link href="/andreasvlachos/" title="Andreas Vlachos" uri="https://mkiyoaki.github.io/andreasvlachos/" display-uri="andreasvlachos" type="local">Andreas Vlachos</fr:link></fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><html:ul><html:li>Investigated post-training dynamics (SFT vs. GRPO) under distributional shifts on Qwen2.5-1.5B using Structural Causal Models and layer-wise linear probing. </html:li>
  <html:li>Demonstrated that outcome-based RL restores syntactic compliance (98.2%) while latent causal rules remain internally encoded (66% probe accuracy) despite generative execution failure.</html:li></html:ul></fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
  

  
    
    
    <fr:tree show-metadata="true" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year></fr:date><fr:uri>https://mkiyoaki.github.io/l193-sparse-feature-circuits/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>l193-sparse-feature-circuits</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/l193-sparse-feature-circuits/</fr:route><fr:title text="Sparse Feature Circuits in Sycophantic Behaviour">Sparse Feature Circuits in Sycophantic Behaviour</fr:title><fr:taxon>Mini Project</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="institution"><fr:link href="https://www.cam.ac.uk/" type="external">University of Cambridge</fr:link> (L193)</fr:meta><fr:meta name="position">Supervisor: Prof. Mateja Jamnik</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><html:ul><html:li>Applied <fr:link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.19647" type="external">Sparse Feature Circuits (SFC) and SHIFT framework</fr:link> with JumpReLU SAEs (Gemma Scope) on Gemma-2-2B to analyze model sycophancy across NLP, Philosophy, and Political domains. </html:li>
  <html:li>Showed that targeted ablation of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[10^2 - 10^3]]></fr:tex> sparse features reduces sycophancy rates from 85.2% to 45.2% with minimal capability degradation.</html:li></html:ul></fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
  

  
    
    
    <fr:tree show-metadata="true" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:date><fr:year>2025</fr:year></fr:date><fr:uri>https://mkiyoaki.github.io/marl-social-dilemmas/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>marl-social-dilemmas</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/marl-social-dilemmas/</fr:route><fr:title text="Multi-Agent RL: Algorithm for Sequential Social Dilemmas">Multi-Agent RL: Algorithm for Sequential Social Dilemmas</fr:title><fr:taxon>Thesis</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="institution"><fr:link href="https://www.kcl.ac.uk/" type="external">King's College London</fr:link></fr:meta><fr:meta name="position">Supervisor: Dr. Milad Kazemi</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><html:ul><html:li>Reproduced and extended MARL pipelines for sequential social dilemmas in <fr:link href="https://github.com/agi-brain/xuance" type="external">XuanCe</fr:link>, including MeltingPot integration, visual-observation support, prosocial reward sharing, and the MOCA contract-augmentation framework.</html:li>
  <html:li>Evaluated multiple MARL methods across MPE, MeltingPot, and CleanUp; MAPPO exceeded the original single-agent PPO baseline on CleanUp by 69.3% (2-agent) and 94.8% (4-agent), with reproduced MOCA yielding additional gains over separate training.</html:li></html:ul></fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
  
